1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,644
MJH proposes three AI-powered decision-support products on a platform.

2
00:00:04,794 --> 00:00:08,718
The suite turns fragmented data into daily recommendations.

3
00:00:09,233 --> 00:00:14,702
The README frames allocation, market trends, and procurement as the core products.

4
00:00:14,852 --> 00:00:19,322
Each product targets a different decision, but shares the same foundation.

5
00:00:19,833 --> 00:00:25,371
The architecture flows from ingestion to canonical data, metrics, and decisions.

6
00:00:25,521 --> 00:00:29,921
That same layer feeds the app, exports, and planner workflows.

7
00:00:30,433 --> 00:00:36,122
The FastAPI app handles sign-in, correlation IDs, and refusal handling.

8
00:00:36,272 --> 00:00:40,521
It also serves the planner app and connects to workflow exports.

9
00:00:41,033 --> 00:00:46,677
The project ships command line tools for database, ingestion, metrics, workflow, and

10
00:00:46,677 --> 00:00:47,349
API tasks.

11
00:00:47,499 --> 00:00:55,533
The database command applies migrations, and the API serves locally on 127.0.0.1.

12
00:00:56,033 --> 00:01:06,715
The acceptance targets are strict, with 98%, 95%, 70%, 95%, and 95% thresholds.

13
00:01:06,865 --> 00:01:10,963
Those targets define the pilot bar before the suite expands.

14
00:01:11,467 --> 00:01:15,263
Clone the repository and explore the shared foundation.

15
00:01:15,413 --> 00:01:19,453
Then try the commands and see how the workflow fits together.
